; The container `for` over something that is not a container.
;
; This case PARSES - `(for x n body)` is a well-formed three-element
; `for` - and is refused by the checker at the user's OWN expression:
; `n` is underlined, not the word `for`. Two rows per site, because the
; desugaring reads the container twice - once for its length, once per
; element - and the checker does not poison a binding after one bad use.
; The first row carries the user's span and the second the keyword's,
; deliberately: two rows with the same message at the same span read as
; a compiler bug rather than as two uses, and that is what giving both
; the user's span produced when it was measured.
;
;   1  a DECLARED `Int` parameter. `expected Vec _a, found Int`.
;   2  an UNDECLARED parameter, which is `Int` and not a type variable -
;      a pre-existing rule about inference without generalisation, and
;      the reason it is written down here is that a reader meeting this
;      diagnostic will otherwise attribute it to `for`.
;
; The file declares its OWN `vecLen`. That is the ablation this case
; exists to hold: the desugaring emits the MANGLED `Vec$vecLen` and
; `Vec$vecGet`, the spelling `mangledFromChain` gives `Vec::vecLen`, so
; a name the entry file declares cannot capture it. Emit a bare
; `vecLen` instead and this file's own wins - the messages become
; `expected Vec Int` and the case goes red. MAC-CAP-10.5 records the
; same defect being real once: an entry file's own `strConcat` captured
; the format lowering's, silently and wrongly.
(import IO)

(:: vecLen (-> (Vec Int) Int))

(fn (vecLen z) 99)

(:: overAnInt (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (overAnInt n)
  (for x n
    (println 1)))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (overAnUndeclaredParam xs)
  (for x xs
    (println 1)))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) 0)
